{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>also from the height.</b> Some say it speaks of the elderly, that they lack strength to ascend to a high place, and they say that the vav of יראו is superfluous. Others say: יראו, his legs." "<b>and the terrors.</b> Doubled peh and lamed, for the ayin is suppressed, and it is from תראו חתת, like פחד." "<b>and the almond blossoms.</b> They said it is from עלתה נצה, and the meaning is: blossomed." "<b>and the grasshopper drags.</b> The ankle. Others say the almond is the organ whose gray hair comes after the head and the beard, and 'the grasshopper drags' is the genitals that pull downward. And others said that the chet stands in place of the ayin — this too is worthless, for one letter is not exchanged for another except for the letters יהו״א, because they are sometimes apparent and sometimes hidden." "<b>and the caper berry fails.</b> They said it is from ולא אבה, and its meaning is the desire of intercourse. And Rabbi Adonim ben Tamim al-Maghribi, of blessed memory, said that אביונה is a designation for the spirit of human beings, and so is its grammatical form: אביון is an adjective for the masculine and אביונה in the feminine, and if you make it diminutive it becomes אבניונה, and likewise the word אמינון, and גשמו means [in place of גשם]. And this is the grammatical reasoning of poverty, for in the sacred language there is no word that has been made diminutive at all, and if this were in the foundation of the language to make diminutives, they would be found in Scripture by the hundreds and thousands. Rather, אמנון and אמינון are two names for one man, like שלמה and שלמון, אבשי, אבישי, אבנר, אבינר — with addition. Others say גשמו is like יתר and יתרו. And now I will explain the verse: after it said 'and those who look through the windows grow dim,' and he neither eats nor hears sound — how can it go back to say 'also from the height they fear' and the matter of the caper? Rather, it is in a different sense: 'also from the height they fear' — his thoughts, for the spirit imagines that it journeys and goes to a high place, and it fears the way, and the end of the verse indicates this, for 'the desire' — the word ינאץ is with alef, and it is like 'and he spurns in the wrath of his anger,' which is like וימאס, even though both are from two different binyanim. And the almond is an adjective on the pattern of ויהי דשן and שן, and its meaning is: watching over the constitution of the body, in the sense of שקדו — they said: שוא שקד, שומר; and likewise נמר שוקד על עריה, 'and his eye spurns to watch.'" "<b>and the grasshopper drags.</b> For it is the failure and the material of the body, and the tav of ויסתבל is from the hitpa'el binyan and is delayed, according to the practice of every samekh and shin, like מסתולל, משתולל." "<b>and the caper berry fails.</b> It is known that the word הפיר always means 'to bring forth,' and the meaning is: the counselor failed in her counsels — and she is the spirit. And this grammatical point Rabbi Judah ben David, of blessed memory, the author of the grammar, already clarified: that the word קם and שב and their cognates have a root of three letters, the middle one being yod, as is indicated by קים in the language of Purim; and the word בינה, like שבתם וקימתם; and הבין, like הקים; and בנת לרעי, like קמת. And the adjective קם, like הנני שב — שבות, and the complete form קים, on the pattern of גנב; and for the feminine, קימה, with heh — milra. I return to the matter of אביונה: the alef is added, like the alef of אקדח and אזרועך and האזניחו, and this word is an adjective from בינה, and it is a designation for the spirit, possessor of understanding." "<b>to his eternal house.</b> That is the grave, and it is the house where he will dwell forever. And there is a finite עולם, like 'and he dwelt there forever' and 'and they shall serve him forever'." "<b>and they go about in the street.</b> When they convey him, they will carry him on the shoulder; they will bear him."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:33.661062454Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}